NOT LANDED CODE. This is a sketch that belongs INSIDE the design document, so a reviewer can argue with the shape. It must never be copied into `src/` by this item — see gate step 1.

The ownership question is the one that decides everything, so the sketch leads with it. WI-9 (plan §11.3) already fixes the precedent shape for per-extension state: `Map<extensionPath, Map<flagName, value>>`, carried at `loader.ts:263-265` today by `extension.flags` (which has `extensionPath`) but NOT by `runtime.flagValues` (which does not — `flagValues.set(name, options.default)` at `:265` is keyed by bare flag name). A state store must be keyed the way `extension.flags` is, not the way `flagValues` is.

```ts
// Sketch for the doc. One store per extension IDENTITY, not per ExtensionContext:
// ExtensionContext is rebuilt on every invocation, so a handle hung off ctx is per-call.
interface ExtensionStateStore {
	get<T>(key: string): T | undefined;
	set<T>(key: string, value: T): void;
	delete(key: string): void;
	keys(): string[];
}

// Proposed surface — ONE new member on ExtensionContext, and the only one this item
// would ever authorise. It is shown here so the review can attack the shape, not so
// anyone can add it today.
interface ExtensionContext {
	// ... 26 existing members ...
	state: ExtensionStateStore;
}

// Host-side, mirroring the §11.3 shape:
//   Map<extensionPath, ExtensionStateStore>
// Unload contract: removing an extension drops ITS entry and nothing else, even when
// another extension previously claimed the same flag name — the earlier claimer's
// value must stay readable. This is WI-9's contract verbatim and the doc should cite
// it rather than reinvent it.
```

The three substrates the doc must rank, with the shape each implies:

(A) Namespaced key-value inside the existing layered `Settings`.
    -> `SettingProvenance` (`config/settings.ts:62`) has six layers, so an overlay gets
       them for free and matches WI-8a's Option A. Cost: near zero. But `Settings` is a
       user-hand-edited YAML file, and plugin-internal state is not user configuration —
       a user who reformats or prunes their settings file would silently delete plugin
       state, and provenance layers that name USER intent (`env`, `runtime`, `overlay`,
       `project`, `global`, `default`) do not describe where a cache entry came from.
       It also inherits a settings lifecycle (no cleanup path) that is the very thing
       being designed around.

(B) A dedicated per-extension store with its own lifecycle and unload cleanup. **Recommended.**
    -> Costs more (a new path helper in `packages/utils/src/dirs.ts` alongside the
       `getPluginsLockfile` pattern at `:652-654`; a new runtime map; a new unload arm in
       WI-9). But the substrate is right: plugin state is not user configuration, so it
       does not belong in a file the user edits, and it can be given the one lifecycle
       property that makes 'clean up what this extension wrote' implementable at all.

(C) What already ships: `pi.appendEntry(<qualified customType>, data)`
    (`ExtensionAPI.appendEntry`, `types.ts:1489`) + rebuild from
    `ctx.sessionManager.getBranch()` on `session_start` / `session_branch` / `session_tree`,
    documented at `docs/extensions.md:699-723`. Session-scoped: entries belong to a
    session branch, so this cannot answer 'remember this across sessions'. Not a
    key-value store — it is append-and-replay, and the latest matching entry wins. The doc
    must state this and must NOT reject it as 'not a store' without saying why, because it
    is the only substrate an author can reach today and the reader will ask.

The doc must also name a concrete on-disk path under (B) and justify it, or it has deferred the decision instead of making it. `~/.omp/extensions-state/<extension-id>.json` is the shape that follows the existing `getPluginsDir()` conventions; the doc either adopts it or replaces it with something better argued. 'We will decide the path later' fails acceptance.
